## The idea

Each cloud is a group of connected "1" cells. Scan the grid; every time we find a cloud cell we have not visited yet, that is a **new cloud** — count it, then "flood fill" from it to mark every cell of that cloud as visited. The flood fill uses our own stack instead of recursion, so a huge solid cloud cannot overflow the call stack. For cells arriving one at a time, **union-find** merges neighbouring clouds in near-constant time.

## Step by step

1. Make a visited grid the same size as the sky (do not change the input).
2. For each cell: if it is cloud and not visited, add 1 to the count and flood-fill from it.
3. Flood fill: push the cell on a stack; pop a cell, look at its 4 neighbours, push every unvisited cloud neighbour. The number of pops is the cloud's size.
4. Largest cloud: same scan, keep the biggest flood-fill size.
5. Streaming (AddCloud): give every cell an id. A new cloud cell starts as its own cloud (count + 1). For each cloud neighbour, if they belong to different clouds, join them and count − 1.

## Worked example

**Input:** 11000 / 11000 / 00100 / 00011

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| (0,0) | new cloud #1 → flood fill marks (0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(1,1) — size 4 |
| (0,1)…(1,1) | already visited → skip |
| (2,2) | new cloud #2 → size 1 |
| (3,3) | new cloud #3 → flood fill marks (3,3),(3,4) — size 2 |

**Result:** 3 clouds; the largest has 4 cells

## Cost

Count and Largest: O(rows × cols) time and memory. AddCloud: almost O(1) per call thanks to union-find with path halving.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// '1' = cloud, '0' = clear sky. Cells touching up/down/left/right belong to the same cloud.
public static class Clouds {

    public static int Count(string[] sky) {
        if (sky == null || sky.Length == 0) return 0;
        bool[,] visited = new bool[sky.Length, sky[0].Length];
        int count = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r < sky.Length; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < sky[0].Length; c++) {
                if (sky[r][c] == '1' && !visited[r, c]) {
                    count++;                          // found a cloud we have not seen yet
                    FloodFill(sky, visited, r, c);    // mark all of it
                }
            }
        }
        return count;
    }

    public static int Largest(string[] sky) {
        if (sky == null || sky.Length == 0) return 0;
        bool[,] visited = new bool[sky.Length, sky[0].Length];
        int best = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r < sky.Length; r++) {
            for (int c = 0; c < sky[0].Length; c++) {
                if (sky[r][c] == '1' && !visited[r, c]) {
                    int size = FloodFill(sky, visited, r, c);
                    if (size > best) best = size;
                }
            }
        }
        return best;
    }

    // Marks every cell of one cloud as visited and returns its size. Uses our own stack, not recursion.
    private static int FloodFill(string[] sky, bool[,] visited, int startRow, int startCol) {
        int rows = sky.Length, cols = sky[0].Length;
        int[] dRow = { 1, -1, 0, 0 };
        int[] dCol = { 0, 0, 1, -1 };
        Stack<int[]> stack = new Stack<int[]>();
        visited[startRow, startCol] = true;
        stack.Push(new int[] { startRow, startCol });
        int size = 0;

        while (stack.Count > 0) {
            int[] cell = stack.Pop();
            size++;
            for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++) {
                int nr = cell[0] + dRow[k];
                int nc = cell[1] + dCol[k];
                bool inside = nr >= 0 && nr < rows && nc >= 0 && nc < cols;
                if (inside && sky[nr][nc] == '1' && !visited[nr, nc]) {
                    visited[nr, nc] = true;            // mark when pushed, so no cell is pushed twice
                    stack.Push(new int[] { nr, nc });
                }
            }
        }
        return size;
    }
}

// Cells become cloudy one at a time. Union-find keeps track of which cloud each cell belongs to.
public class CloudTracker {
    private readonly int _rows, _cols;
    private readonly int[] _parent;     // _parent[i] == i means i is the "leader" of its cloud
    private readonly bool[] _isCloud;
    public int CloudCount { get; private set; }

    public CloudTracker(int rows, int cols) {
        if (rows <= 0 || cols <= 0) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException();
        _rows = rows;
        _cols = cols;
        _parent = new int[rows * cols];
        _isCloud = new bool[rows * cols];
        for (int i = 0; i < _parent.Length; i++) _parent[i] = i;
    }

    public int AddCloud(int r, int c) {
        if (r < 0 || c < 0 || r >= _rows || c >= _cols) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException();
        int id = r * _cols + c;
        if (_isCloud[id]) return CloudCount;          // already cloud: nothing changes

        _isCloud[id] = true;
        CloudCount++;                                  // starts as a new, separate cloud

        int[] dRow = { 1, -1, 0, 0 };
        int[] dCol = { 0, 0, 1, -1 };
        for (int k = 0; k < 4; k++) {
            int nr = r + dRow[k], nc = c + dCol[k];
            if (nr < 0 || nc < 0 || nr >= _rows || nc >= _cols) continue;
            int neighbour = nr * _cols + nc;
            if (!_isCloud[neighbour]) continue;
            int a = Find(id), b = Find(neighbour);
            if (a != b) {                              // two different clouds touch: join them
                _parent[a] = b;
                CloudCount--;
            }
        }
        return CloudCount;
    }

    // Follow parents to the leader. "Path halving" shortens the chain as we go, keeping it fast.
    private int Find(int x) {
        while (_parent[x] != x) {
            _parent[x] = _parent[_parent[x]];
            x = _parent[x];
        }
        return x;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Empty grid? Ragged rows? Diagonal neighbours?**

0; reject; ask — 4 or 8 connectivity

**Q: Complexity?**

O(rows × cols) time; visited array

**Q: Cells arrive from several satellites at once.**

Union-find under one lock, or partition the grid

**Q: A 100,000 × 100,000 sky.**

Process in tiles and merge components on the borders

**Q: How would you test it?**

Random grids against a simple reference; a large solid cloud for stack depth
